WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.520
Most mobile text entry methods for blind

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.340
users depend on existing braille skills, making

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.700
them impractical for those without braille literacy.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.940
Others create device-specific approaches that can't

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.760
transfer across platforms, or rely on external

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.780
hardware that is inconvenient to carry.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:20.300
We introduce BezelBraille, a novel, eyes-free,

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.940
braille-inspired keyboard designed for blind and

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.960
low-vision users of all braille proficiency

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:25.520
levels.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.720
BezelBraille maps the six dots of a

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.820
braille cell onto angular layouts at the

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.040
bottom corners of a phone.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.160
In each corner, the top dot is

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.940
above the corner, the middle dot at

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.660
the corner, and the bottom dot on

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:38.160
the side.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.580
To enter a character, users connect the

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.140
dots of the left or right column

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.100
with their thumbs, either sequentially or simultaneously.

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:51.010
The phone's bezel and corners act as

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.830
tactile landmarks, helping users keep orientation.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:57.410
Gestures are direction- and size-agnostic,

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.670
thus any stroke passing through the zones

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.410
counts, eliminating the need for precise targeting.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.910
Common editing gestures are also supported.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.770
Swipe left for backspace, right for space,

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:10.890
up for enter, and down for shift.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.830
To support learning, letters are first spoken

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:14.270
aloud.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.410
If correct, users swipe up to confirm

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:17.830
input.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:20.950
Once familiar, this step can be skipped

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:21.930
for faster typing.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.130
We designed the keyboard through a focus

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.250
group and evaluated it with 12 blind

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.670
participants of varying braille skills over 8

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:29.250
sessions.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:32.510
Typing speed rose from 3 words per

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:34.290
minute in the first session to 4.2

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:36.570
by the last, after only short

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:37.130
practice.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.130
Without the confirmation step, speeds increased to

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.750
6.9 words per minute, with projections

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.350
of 25.5 after 10 hours of

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:46.690
use.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:51.830
Importantly, braille proficiency itself improved significantly, from

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:54.650
12% to 79% among novices.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.230
Participants described bezel braille as easy to

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:01.130
learn, accurate, and practical for everyday use,

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:03.450
highlighting its potential as both a text

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.650
entry solution and a braille learning tool.
